   Sacramento, CA, MAY 21-25, 2017},
Organization = {Amer Soc Civil Engineers; Amer Soc Civil Engineers, Environm \& Water
   Resources Inst},
Abstract = {This paper presents the results regarding an experimental and numerical
   study about a buried PVC pipe and its interaction with the surrounding
   soil. The interaction between a buried pipe and the composite soil has
   been simulated by using elastoplastic finite element models to determine
   the levels of deformation in the pipe and settlement in the soil. A
   series of loading configuration tests were conducted, using a prototype
   trench, in which a flexible pipeline was buried in composite soil layers
   of known properties. The resulting data from these tests were used to
   validate output mathematical models using a Mohr Coulomb Plasticity
   model in one case, and a Dragger Prager model in the second to simulate
   the stress-strain behaviours of a composite soil and a buried PVC pipe.
   The pipe-soil interactions were found to be influenced by the pattern of
   soil and bedding layers, thereby establishing that the properties of the
   soil play a significant role in determining levels of deformation and
   displacement of a sewer pipe cross-section. The use of ABAQUS, a finite
   elements package, and selection of the appropriate model to simulate
   soil elastoplastic behaviour, has confirmed the importance, significance
   and sensitivity of the soil material properties on the numerical
   simulation accuracy when compared with experimental data.},
